  "articleBody": "Trying to fix a wobbly handrail yourself can be tempting, but it’s easy to make mistakes that actually worsen the problem or leave it unsafe. First, don't use just any fixing. The type of wall material—whether it's drywall, plaster, or masonry—dictates the correct fasteners you need. Using the wrong ones means the rail won't be secure for long. Second, never reuse stripped screw holes; they won't hold. You'll need to either relocate the bracket slightly or repair the hole properly. Third, don't overlook hidden looseness. Sometimes, the movement isn't just at a visible bracket; it could be a compromised newel post or baluster connection. Finally, always load-test the rail after you think it's fixed. Give it a good push and pull, just like someone would when using it for support. Skipping this step means you won't truly know if it's stable. If you're unsure about any of these steps, especially with masonry or damaged framing, it’s always best to call in someone with experience. HH Remodeling LLC has the expertise to make sure your handrail is not just repaired, but genuinely safe and secure.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
